},{"x":0.22761,"y":0.57841,"w":0.01963,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"180"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.57974,"w":0.59315,"h":0.09038,"fs":0.01279,"n":7,"k":1,"t":"Solution From the Pythagorean theorem and given information, we compute an approxi- mate hypothetical flight distance of 179 mi, had the plane been flying along the original correct course (see Figure 1.69). Knowing the flight distance from Chicago to St. Louis, we next calculate the remaining leg of the original course to be 61 mi. Applying the Pythagorean theorem again then gives an approximate distance of 62 mi from the position of the plane to St. Louis. Finally, from Figure 1.69, we see that 180 sin a = 12 and 62 sin b = 12, so"},{"x":0.10493,"y":0.59439,"w":0.07934,"h":0.02217,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"62 61 12"},{"x":0.04865,"y":0.6139,"w":0.21069,"h":0.01918,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Plane position St. Louis"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.67933,"w":0.25471,"h":0.04297,"fs":0.01151,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 1.69 Diagram for drift correc- tion (Example 9), with distances surrounded to the nearest mile (drawing not to scale)."},{"x":0.50408,"y":0.6801,"w":0.08666,"h":0.01982,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"a = sin-1 12"},{"x":0.57031,"y":0.68645,"w":0.18866,"h":0.02068,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"180 ≈0.067 radian ≈3.8°"},{"x":0.50409,"y":0.71309,"w":0.08257,"h":0.01982,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"b = sin-112"},{"x":0.57031,"y":0.71944,"w":0.18866,"h":0.02076,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"62 ≈0.195 radian ≈11.2°"},{"x":0.50503,"y":0.74566,"w":0.12647,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"c = a + b ≈15°."},{"x":0.05009,"y":0.78215,"w":0.05269,"h":0.01216,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"cos–1(−x)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.78307,"w":0.3214,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Identities Involving Arcsine and Arccosine"},{"x":0.18231,"y":0.80561,"w":0.03307,"h":0.01216,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"cos–1x"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.80772,"w":0.46627,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"As we can see from Figure 1.70, the arccosine of x satisfies the identity"},{"x":0.53241,"y":0.82887,"w":0.37984,"h":0.01467,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"cos-1x + cos-1(-x) = p, (3)"},{"x":0.05911,"y":0.84148,"w":0.13866,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"0 −x x −1 1"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.85377,"w":0.01363,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"or"},{"x":0.53136,"y":0.87491,"w":0.38088,"h":0.01467,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"cos-1(-x) = p - cos-1x. (4)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.89913,"w":0.42253,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Also, we can see from the triangle in Figure 1.71 that for x 7 0,"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.90657,"w":0.25609,"h":0.02855,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 1.70 cos-1x and cos-1(-x) are supplementary angles (so their sum is p)."},{"x":0.53511,"y":0.91239,"w":0.37714,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"sin-1x + cos-1x = p>2. (5)"}]}